1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,350
Mrs.

2
00:00:00,967 --> 00:00:05,548
Rao buys her Laddoo Lab six-pack
every week, without a second thought.

3
00:00:05,848 --> 00:00:10,013
But for her daughter's new school bag,
she visited four shops and compared

4
00:00:10,013 --> 00:00:10,913
every zip.

5
00:00:11,154 --> 00:00:12,139
Same customer.

6
00:00:12,139 --> 00:00:14,265
Two very different ways of buying.

7
00:00:14,265 --> 00:00:15,165
Why?

8
00:00:15,384 --> 00:00:18,829
Zoya spots this while
Arjun pitches a local shop.

9
00:00:19,179 --> 00:00:21,718
People buy different
things in different ways.

10
00:00:22,069 --> 00:00:23,573
Some things they grab.

11
00:00:23,573 --> 00:00:24,832
Some they compare.

12
00:00:24,832 --> 00:00:26,195
Some they hunt for.

13
00:00:26,195 --> 00:00:28,978
And some they never
think of buying at all.

14
00:00:29,628 --> 00:00:31,550
That is product classification.

15
00:00:31,850 --> 00:00:36,165
It means sorting products into groups,
by how and why people buy them.

16
00:00:36,515 --> 00:00:42,090
In 1923, Melvin Copeland of Harvard
sorted consumer goods into three types:

17
00:00:42,090 --> 00:00:44,913
convenience, shopping and specialty.

18
00:00:45,263 --> 00:00:48,404
Today's textbooks add a
fourth: unsought products.

19
00:00:48,754 --> 00:00:53,007
And there is a second family: industrial
products, which businesses buy

20
00:00:53,007 --> 00:00:54,828
to run and to make things.

21
00:00:55,478 --> 00:00:57,412
Let's sort four things Mrs.

22
00:00:57,412 --> 00:01:00,141
Rao buys, with three simple questions.

23
00:01:00,491 --> 00:01:02,123
First, the laddoo six-pack.

24
00:01:02,123 --> 00:01:03,548
Is she looking for it?

25
00:01:03,548 --> 00:01:04,448
Yes.

26
00:01:04,339 --> 00:01:06,320
Will only one special kind do?

27
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:07,220
No.

28
00:01:06,752 --> 00:01:08,292
Does she compare first?

29
00:01:08,292 --> 00:01:09,192
No.

30
00:01:08,724 --> 00:01:10,029
She just grabs it.

31
00:01:10,029 --> 00:01:11,466
A convenience product.

32
00:01:11,816 --> 00:01:13,347
Next, the school bag.

33
00:01:13,347 --> 00:01:16,988
She wants one, and she compares
price, quality and style.

34
00:01:16,988 --> 00:01:18,277
A shopping product.

35
00:01:18,627 --> 00:01:20,947
Then, a silk saree for a wedding.

36
00:01:20,947 --> 00:01:24,850
She will only buy from one
weaver she trusts, even far away.

37
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:26,221
A specialty product.

38
00:01:26,571 --> 00:01:28,108
Last, life insurance.

39
00:01:28,108 --> 00:01:32,476
She is not looking for it at all,
until someone explains why she needs

40
00:01:32,476 --> 00:01:33,376
it.

41
00:01:32,924 --> 00:01:34,315
An unsought product.

42
00:01:34,665 --> 00:01:36,447
Four products, four bins.

43
00:01:36,447 --> 00:01:39,131
Each one needs a different marketing plan.

44
00:01:39,781 --> 00:01:42,315
How people buy decides
how you should sell.

45
00:01:43,015 --> 00:01:45,522
Here is what each type
means for marketing.

46
00:01:45,872 --> 00:01:48,637
Convenience products are
cheap and bought often.

47
00:01:48,637 --> 00:01:50,898
Sell them everywhere, close to home.

48
00:01:51,248 --> 00:01:53,649
Shopping products are bought less often.

49
00:01:53,649 --> 00:01:56,244
Give people facts, and help them compare.

50
00:01:56,594 --> 00:01:59,848
Specialty products have
loyal buyers who will travel.

51
00:01:59,848 --> 00:02:02,072
Fewer shops, and a strong brand.

52
00:02:02,422 --> 00:02:04,536
Unsought products need explaining.

53
00:02:04,536 --> 00:02:08,418
People must be told why they
need them, often face to face.

54
00:02:09,068 --> 00:02:11,236
Laddoo Lab is also a buyer.

55
00:02:11,236 --> 00:02:13,326
It buys industrial products.

56
00:02:13,675 --> 00:02:15,716
Ragi and jaggery are materials.

57
00:02:15,716 --> 00:02:17,815
They become part of the laddoo.

58
00:02:18,165 --> 00:02:20,292
The grinder is a capital item.

59
00:02:20,292 --> 00:02:22,994
It is costly, and it lasts for years.

60
00:02:23,344 --> 00:02:26,079
And packing tape and
cooking gas are supplies.

61
00:02:26,079 --> 00:02:28,353
They get used up, and bought again.

62
00:02:29,003 --> 00:02:30,970
Where do we see this at work today?

63
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:32,245
Convenience.

64
00:02:32,245 --> 00:02:35,702
A biscuit maker puts its
packs in every small shop.

65
00:02:36,052 --> 00:02:36,952
Shopping.

66
00:02:36,886 --> 00:02:40,154
A phone maker shows side
by side comparisons.

67
00:02:40,504 --> 00:02:41,423
Specialty.

68
00:02:41,423 --> 00:02:44,984
A luxury watchmaker sells
in only a few stores.

69
00:02:45,334 --> 00:02:46,234
Unsought.

70
00:02:46,179 --> 00:02:50,222
An insurance company sends
advisers to explain its plans.

71
00:02:50,872 --> 00:02:52,895
But these boxes are not fixed.

72
00:02:53,245 --> 00:02:54,197
The good side.

73
00:02:54,197 --> 00:02:57,155
They tell you where to
sell, and how hard to push.

74
00:02:57,505 --> 00:02:58,597
The hard side.

75
00:02:58,597 --> 00:03:02,617
The same product can sit in
different boxes for different people.

76
00:03:02,967 --> 00:03:03,867
For Mrs.

77
00:03:03,735 --> 00:03:06,099
Rao, Laddoo Lab is a quick grab.

78
00:03:06,099 --> 00:03:10,987
For a fan who crosses the city just
for Nani's laddoos, it is a specialty.

79
00:03:11,337 --> 00:03:12,252
The lesson?

80
00:03:12,252 --> 00:03:15,634
Classify by how people
buy, not by what you make.

81
00:03:16,284 --> 00:03:17,250
Quick check.

82
00:03:17,250 --> 00:03:21,701
A family visits five shops and
compares fridges on price and features.

83
00:03:21,701 --> 00:03:24,112
What type of product is the fridge?

84
00:03:26,012 --> 00:03:26,912
Shopping!

85
00:03:26,745 --> 00:03:30,353
They compare price, quality
and features before they buy.

86
00:03:30,953 --> 00:03:31,962
So, remember.

87
00:03:32,262 --> 00:03:36,683
Product classification sorts products
by how and why people buy them.

88
00:03:37,033 --> 00:03:41,119
Consumer products are convenience,
shopping, specialty and unsought.

89
00:03:41,469 --> 00:03:46,563
Businesses buy industrial products:
materials, capital items and supplies.

90
00:03:47,163 --> 00:03:51,699
Next time, we'll look inside one
product, layer by layer: product levels

91
00:03:51,699 --> 00:03:53,456
and the product hierarchy.
